  overview="To Be Young, Gifted and Black is a portrait of Lorraine Hansberry in her own words, adapted by Robert Nemiroff from her plays, letters, journals, speeches and essays. It was staged in New York in 1969 and published in book form with an introduction by James Baldwin.",
  content="A collage of passages from Hansberry's writings, including her childhood memories of Chicago, letters, diary entries, speeches on the Black writer and scenes from her plays, arranged to tell the story of her life and thought.",
  summary="The work traces Hansberry's growth from a Chicago childhood marked by segregation, through her political education in New York and the success of A Raisin in the Sun, to her final illness, letting her voice speak through her writings. Its title comes from a speech Hansberry gave in May 1964 to the young winners of a creative-writing competition sponsored by the United Negro College Fund; it also became the title of Nina Simone's 1969 civil rights song.",
  themes_text="The artist's vocation and responsibility to her time; Black identity and pride; political commitment across lines of race and nation; and the urgency that early death gives to all of these.",
